# ============================================================
# YELP CLUSTER. ALL 302, AND THE STATUS IS THE POINT.
# ============================================================
#
# THESE ARE TEMPORARY AND THE ARTICLES ARE COMING BACK TO THESE EXACT PATHS.
# Four of the five slugs are members of ROOT_URL_SLUGS in
# tools/eleventy/article-directory-data.js, which is the closed set of migrated
# pieces that publish at root rather than under /yelp/. When those articles land
# they land here, at the same URL that is redirecting away today.
#
# 301 WOULD BE THE WRONG INSTRUMENT AND WOULD OUTLIVE ITS USEFULNESS. A browser
# caches a permanent redirect and keeps honouring it after the rule is gone from
# this file, so a returning reader would be sent away from an article that exists.
# The build assertion cannot reach a cache. 302 keeps the URL in the index,
# preserves the signal that /is-yelp-advertising-worth-it alone carries at
# roughly 35,000 impressions, and costs nothing to unwind.
#
# THE BUILD REMOVES THE NEED TO REMEMBER. The day any of these articles
# publishes, its rule becomes a rule whose source is a built page, and
# eleventy.config.js fails the build naming both. That is the guard; nothing here
# depends on a human noticing months from now.
#
# /yelp-faqs is the one that is not a ROOT_URL_SLUGS member, because the FAQ is
# a directory of many files rather than one article returning to one path. It is
# still 302: src/yelp/faq/ does not exist yet, a fifty question FAQ is planned,
# and that path is a plausible home for it.
/is-yelp-advertising-worth-it   /yelp/  302
/why-does-yelp-filter-reviews   /yelp/  302
/yelp-enhanced-profile          /yelp/  302
/yelp-portfolios-optimization   /yelp/  302
/yelp-faqs                      /yelp/  302

